Assistant: The determinant of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix is $$\begin{vmatrix} 1 & \cos \theta \\ \cos \theta & 1 \end{vmatrix} = \cos^2 \theta - \cos^2 \theta = 0.$$ In other words, the given determinant is 0 for all positive values of $\theta$. For $\sin \theta$, we know that $\sin (-\theta) = - \sin \theta$, so our determinant becomes 
$$\begin{vmatrix} 1 & \cos \theta & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + \sin \theta & 1 \\ 1 + \sin \theta & 1 & 1 \end{vmatrix} = \begin{vmatrix} 1 & \cos \theta & 1 \\ 1 & \cos \theta & 1 \\ 1 & 0  & 1 \end{vmatrix}
$$
The determinant of the last matrix is $$1 \cdot 1 \cdot 1 - \cos \theta \cdot \cos \theta \cdot 1 = 1 - \cos^2 \theta = 1 - \frac{1}{2} ( \cos 2\theta - 2) \\ = \frac{1+ \cos 2\theta}{2}.$$ We know that $1+ \cos (2\theta)= \cos^2 \theta + \cos \theta \sin \theta + \cos \theta \sin \theta + \cos^2 \theta$. Thus, the value of our determinant is \[\frac{1}{2} (1+ \cos (2\theta) + \cos^2 \theta) \\ = \frac{2}{2} +\frac{1}{2} \cos \theta, \] so the maximum value of the determinant is 3. \boxed{3}

